    firstBody: `Once per brand, at any size. It is what the pilot package used to be, without the fixed count: ${WORKED[1]} complete products is ${euro(firstEg.listTotal, 'en')} ${vatLabel('excl', 'en')}, and on a first order that is ${euro(firstEg.net, 'en')} ${vatLabel('excl', 'en')}.`,
    ladderCta: 'Start an order',

    anchorH: 'What this replaces',
    anchorWasTag: 'A production day',
    anchorNowTag: `${ANCHOR_PRODUCTS} products here`,
    anchorNowMeta: 'Catalog set and lifestyle carousel for each, one briefing, one invoice',
    anchorPerProduct: `${euro(ladderRate('complete', ANCHOR_PRODUCTS), 'en')} per product at that count · ${vatLabel('excl', 'en')}`,
    anchorNowList: [
      'Nothing to ship, nowhere to travel, no day off the business',
      'The output is defined before you pay for it',
      'Run it again next season without rebooking anything',
    ],
    // The last sentence carries the only body link on this page to /compare —
    // the page that runs this same comparison at full length. It is written
    // into the note rather than added as a related-page block because this is
    // the exact paragraph where a reader starts wanting the rest of it.
    anchorNote: `Neither figure tells you what actually comes out of a shoot day — that depends on the day. ${shoot.caveat} What an order here fixes is that its output is agreed before you pay for it. The rest of that comparison — the hours, the risk, what each one leaves you holding — is <a href="${lp('/compare')}">set out side by side</a>.`,

    addOnH: 'Added to an order',
    addOnSub: 'Neither of these is a way to buy visuals on its own. Both sit on top of an order you are already running.',
    brandModelName: 'Your Brand Model',
    brandModelLine: 'One face. Every order. Only yours.',
    brandModelUnit: 'one-time setup',
    brandModelCta: 'See Your Brand Model',
    // FigPayback's three labels. They are copy, so they live in the COPY table
    // and travel through check_lang_parity like every other string; the drawing
    // itself holds no language and no number. The VAT label rides on the plate
    // label because the plate is where the figure is: the drawing prints a
    // price, so the drawing says which price it is.
    pbTotalLabel: `Setup, ${vatLabel('excl', 'en')}`,
    // The credit cells print a price too, so the run label carries the label
