    ],
    anchorNote: `Geen van beide bedragen zegt iets over wat er uit een shootdag komt — dat hangt van de dag af. ${shoot.caveat} Wat een bestelling hier vastlegt, is dat de uitkomst vaststaat voordat je betaalt. De rest van die vergelijking — de uren, het risico, wat je aan elk van beide overhoudt — staat <a href="${lp('/compare')}">naast elkaar uitgeschreven</a>.`,

    addOnH: 'Toe te voegen aan een bestelling',
    addOnSub: 'Geen van beide is op zichzelf een manier om visuals te kopen. Ze komen bovenop een bestelling die je al draait.',
    brandModelName: 'Jouw merkmodel',
    brandModelLine: 'Eén gezicht. Elke bestelling. Alleen van jou.',
    brandModelUnit: 'eenmalige setup',
    brandModelCta: 'Bekijk Jouw merkmodel',
    pbTotalLabel: `Setup, ${vatLabel('excl', 'nl')}`,
    pbRunLabel: `Verrekend met elk van je eerste ${BRAND_MODEL_CREDIT_DROPS} bestellingen · ${vatLabel('excl', 'nl')}`,
    pbEndLabel: 'Setup terugverdiend',
    videoName: 'Videoclip',
    videoLine: 'Eén korte clip. Dezelfde prijs los of toegevoegd aan elke bestelling.',
    videoUnit: 'per clip',
    videoIncH: 'Hoe het geprijsd is:',
    videoList: [
      'Binnen een bestelling hetzelfde tarief als los — het is hetzelfde werk',
      `${PLAN_CLIPS.studio} clips per maand zitten in het ${planById.studio.name}-plan`,
      'Gemaakt van dezelfde productfoto’s als de rest van de bestelling',
    ],
    videoCta: 'Bekijk wat een clip is',

    inclH: 'In elke bestelling',
    svcLead: 'Het serviceniveau volgt de omvang.',
    svcBody: `Vanaf ${WINDOW_THRESHOLD} producten gaat een bestelling in de capaciteitsagenda en krijgt hij het gereserveerde venster. Daaronder loopt hij in de standaard wachtrij. Eén grens om over te gaan, in plaats van een tweede vraag om te beantwoorden.`,
    svcFromTag: `Vanaf ${WINDOW_THRESHOLD} producten`,
    svcUnderTag: `Onder ${WINDOW_THRESHOLD} producten`,
    gateLead: 'Er wordt geen datum genoemd voordat de agenda die heeft vrijgegeven.',
    gateBody: 'Een gereserveerd venster wordt bevestigd voordat je betaalt, nooit erna. Kan de week die je nodig hebt niet worden vastgehouden, dan hoor je dat — in plaats van een optimistische gok.',

    plansH: 'De plannen',
    plansSub: 'Komt dezelfde output elke maand terug, dan is per maand kopen goedkoper dan bestelling voor bestelling. Meer is een plan niet — geen groter pakket, dezelfde producten voor een lagere prijs omdat je je aan de maand verbindt. Elke kaart laat het verschil met de staffel hierboven zien.',
    plansCta: 'Een plan opzetten',

    sampleH: 'Voordat je iets vastlegt',
    sampleBody: `Eén product, ${sample.price} ${vatLabel('excl', 'nl')} — ${sample.unit}. Het loopt door dezelfde pipeline als een betaalde bestelling, dus wat terugkomt is wat je zou krijgen.`,
    // NO VAT LABEL ON A BUTTON. A CTA is a label, not a price quotation, and
    // the site's rule — every printed PRICE carries excl. VAT — is about the
    // figures a customer compares, not about the word on a button. It also
    // overflowed: the Dutch string ran to 363.6px in a 360px viewport. The
    // €0,99 keeps its label in sampleBody just above, where it sits among
    // other figures and the label means something.
    sampleCta: `Vraag een proefvisual aan · ${sample.price}`,

    faqH: 'Vragen over prijzen',

    ctaH1: 'Zie het op je',
    ctaEm: 'eigen product.',
    ctaSample: `Proefvisual · ${sample.price} ${vatLabel('excl', 'nl')}`,
    ctaStart: 'Start een bestelling',
    ctaWa: 'WhatsApp',
    waText: 'Hoi%20VISUAILS%2C%20ik%20heb%20een%20vraag%20over%20de%20prijzen.',
  },
};

const c = { ...COPY[lang], faqs: pricingFaqs(lang) };

// What is true of EVERY order, whatever its size — read from the tier rather
// than typed, and read from the SMALLER tier on purpose.
//
// Under the packages this list described an attended order, because an
// attended order was the only thing the top of the page sold. The ladder sells
// one product at a time upward, so a promise printed here has to hold for an
// order of one. reviewClaim() and aftercare() are identical on both tiers
// today — that is the point of them, section 13's "a different service model,
// not a worse product" — but reading them off `unattended` means this list
// stays true by construction if that ever stops being the case, instead of
// quietly over-promising to the smallest orders.
const included = [
  reviewClaim('unattended', lang),
  aftercare('unattended', lang),
  lang === 'nl'
    ? 'Hoge resolutie en e-commerce-klaar, zonder licentie per gebruik'
    : 'High-resolution and e-commerce-ready, with no per-use licence',
  lang === 'nl'
    ? 'Eén briefing, één factuur, ongeacht het aantal'
    : 'One briefing, one invoice, whatever the count',
  lang === 'nl'
    ? 'Hetzelfde tarief op elk product in de bestelling'
    : 'The same rate on every product in the order',
];

// The three facts that DIFFER by order size, side by side.
//
// Not all five TIER_ROWS: `review` and `aftercare` are identical on both tiers
// and are stated once in `included` above instead. A comparison table exists to
// show a difference, and a row that reads the same on both sides invites the
// reader to hunt for the difference that is not there — worse here than
// elsewhere, because the two rows in question are the two the smaller order
// most needs to be believed about.
const SVC_KEYS = ['turnaround', 'queue', 'delivery'];
const svcLabels = tierRowLabels(lang, SVC_KEYS);
const svcRows = TIER_ROWS.filter((r) => SVC_KEYS.includes(r.key));
---

<!-- HERO -->
<section class="page-hero"><div class="container">
  <span class="eyebrow-page">{c.eyebrow}</span>
  <h1 class="display">{c.h1}</h1>
  <p class="lead" style="margin-top:1.2rem">{c.lead}</p>
</div></section>

<!-- THE LADDER.
     A real table, because this is a rate card and a rate card is tabular data:
     a reader who wants to know what 12 lifestyle-only products cost should be
     able to find the row and the column and stop reading. Cards would have
     forced one card per rung per kind — fifteen of them — which is how the old
     page ended up with two packages and a hole between them.

     Every cell prints the net rate large and the VAT-inclusive rate under it.
     Fifteen pairs is a lot of small type, and it is still the right call: this
     is the one table on the site a Dutch business will read with a calculator,
     and the alternative — one footnote saying "all prices excl. VAT" — is the
     typed string BRIEF-14 exists to remove. -->
<section class="section-tight"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-head"><h2>{c.ladderH}</h2><p>{c.ladderSub}</p></div>

  <div class="ladder-wrap reveal pending">
    <table class="ladder">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col" class="lad-corner">{c.ladderProductsCol}</th>
          {KIND_IDS.map((id) => (
            <th scope="col">
              <span class="lad-kind">
                {KIND_PAGE[id] ? <a href={lp(KIND_PAGE[id] as string)}>{c.kinds[id].name}</a> : c.kinds[id].name}
              </span>
              <span class="lad-kind-sub">{c.kinds[id].sub}</span>
              <span class="lad-kind-vat">{c.perProduct} · {vatLabel('excl', lang)}</span>
            </th>
          ))}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {rungs.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{r.label}</th>
            {KIND_IDS.map((id) => (
              <td>
                <span class="lad-rate">{euro(ladderRate(id, r.at), lang)}</span>
              </td>
            ))}
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <!-- The multiplication, done. Three counts, and the middle one carries the
       grid's one coloured plate — the fence in global.css is at most two per
       grid and this page spends one. It is on the middle count because that is
       the count the old model had no price for. -->
  <div class="worked-head"><h3>{c.workedH}</h3><p>{c.workedSub}</p></div>
  <div class="tiles worked reveal-group">
    {WORKED.map((n, i) => {
      const q = quote('complete', n);
      return (
        <div class={`tile s-4 reveal pending${i === 1 ? ' on-bright tile-c-blue' : ''}`}>
          <div class="tile-body">
            <p class="tile-k">{n} {c.workedUnit}</p>
            <p class="tile-v">{euro(q.net, lang)}</p>
            <p class="worked-vat">{vatLabel('excl', lang)}</p>
            <p class="tile-n">{euro(q.rate, lang)} {c.perProduct} · {vatLabel('excl', lang)}</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      );
    })}
  </div>

  <!-- The first-order discount, stated once. It is the whole of what the Drop
       Pilot was, so it gets the Pilot's old job: one reason to place a first
       order, at any size, with nothing to explain beyond the sentence. -->
  <p class="gate-note first-note reveal pending"><strong>{c.firstLead}</strong> {c.firstBody}</p>

  <div class="flex ladder-cta">
    <a href={lp('/start')} class="btn btn-primary">{c.ladderCta}</a>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- THE ANCHOR — what a production day costs, itemised rather than narrowed.
     AUDIT-TASK-0.md §H flag 2: the range is the brief's figure and is not
     sourced, so it is presented as a full day with every line counted, the
     lines are named without inventing a euro figure for any of them, and the
     caveat travels with it. No strikethrough: crossing out someone else's
     price is a discount-site gesture, and the comparison is strong enough
     stated plainly.

     BOTH COLUMNS CARRY A VAT LABEL, including the one that is not our price.
     A net figure set against a gross one is a comparison that flatters us by
     a fifth, and the reader cannot see that it has happened. Saying "excl."
     under both is the only way the two columns are the same kind of number. -->
<section class="section-tight"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-head"><h2>{c.anchorH}</h2></div>

  <div class="anchor reveal pending">
    <div class="anchor-col">
      <span class="anchor-tag">{c.anchorWasTag}</span>
      <span class="anchor-price">{shoot.range}</span>
      <span class="anchor-vat">{vatLabel('excl', lang)}</span>
      <p class="anchor-meta">{shoot.basis}</p>
      <ul class="anchor-list">
        {shoot.items.map((it) => <li>{it}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="anchor-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <div class="anchor-col">
      <span class="anchor-tag">{c.anchorNowTag}</span>
      <span class="anchor-price">{euro(quote('complete', ANCHOR_PRODUCTS).net, lang)}</span>
      <span class="anchor-vat">{vatLabel('excl', lang)}</span>
      <p class="anchor-meta">{c.anchorNowMeta}</p>
      <ul class="anchor-list">
        {c.anchorNowList.map((it) => <li>{it}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p class="anchor-per">{c.anchorPerProduct}</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="anchor-note reveal pending" set:html={c.anchorNote} />
</div></section>

<!-- WHAT RIDES ON TOP OF AN ORDER.
     Two products, two shapes, and the asymmetry is the argument. A Brand Model
     is a one-time price whose interesting property is arithmetic: it is exactly
     five credits, so it cancels itself over five orders. That is drawn, because
     a drawing is how you show a sum. A video clip is a rate whose interesting
     property is that it does not move — the same figure inside an order as on
     its own — and a rate that does not move is a short list of the places it
     does not move in. Neither layout would work for the other product, which
     is the test this section used to fail when both were one .map().

     WHAT THE BRAND MODEL BLOCK DOES NOT CARRY, on purpose: its four inclusions.
     They are one click away on /custom-models, which is a page about the
     product; this is a page about the money, and the four bullets were the
     third statement of the credit on a page that already had two. -->
<section class="section-tight"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-head"><h2>{c.addOnH}</h2><p>{c.addOnSub}</p></div>

  <div class="addon reveal pending">
    <div class="addon-id">
      <h3 class="addon-name">{c.brandModelName}</h3>
      <p class="addon-line">{c.brandModelLine}</p>
    </div>
    <p class="addon-price">
      <span class="ap-amount">{euro(AMOUNT.brandModel, lang)}</span>
      <span class="ap-unit">{c.brandModelUnit}</span>
      <span class="ap-vat">{vatLabel('excl', lang)}</span>
    </p>

    <div class="addon-fig">
      <FigPayback
        totalLabel={c.pbTotalLabel}
        total={euro(AMOUNT.brandModel, lang)}
        credit={euro(AMOUNT.brandModelCredit, lang)}
        steps={BRAND_MODEL_CREDIT_DROPS}
        runLabel={c.pbRunLabel}
        endLabel={c.pbEndLabel}
      />
    </div>

    <a href={lp('/custom-models')} class="link-arrow addon-cta">{c.brandModelCta}
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="i"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg>
    </a>
  </div>

  <div class="standing reveal pending">
    <div class="standing-id">
      <h3 class="standing-name"><a href={lp('/video')}>{c.videoName}</a></h3>
      <p class="standing-line">{c.videoLine}</p>
      <p class="standing-price">
        <span class="sp-amount">{euro(AMOUNT.video, lang)}</span>
        <span class="sp-unit">{c.videoUnit}</span>
        <span class="sp-vat">{vatLabel('excl', lang)}</span>
      </p>
      <a href={lp('/video')} class="link-arrow standing-cta">{c.videoCta}
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="i"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg>
      </a>
    </div>
    <div class="standing-inc">
      <span class="standing-inc-h">{c.videoIncH}</span>
      <ul class="standing-list">
        {c.videoList.map((f) => <li>{f}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- WHAT EVERY ORDER INCLUDES, AND WHERE THE RESERVED WINDOW STARTS -->
<section class="section-tight on-ink"><div class="container">
  <h2>{c.inclH}</h2>
  <ul class="incl-grid reveal-group">
    {included.map((item) => (
      <li class="incl reveal pending">{item}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  {/* The old page asked this as a second question — pick a size, then pick a
      service level — and priced the two axes on one list. Size decides it now,
      so this block states the line rather than offering a choice, and the
      three facts that actually change across it are shown side by side. The
      cheaper side is set at the same size, in the same frame, with the same
      type: it is a different service model, not a lesser one, and a thinner
      column would say "lesser" without using any of the words section 13
      bans. */}
  <p class="svc-lead"><strong>{c.svcLead}</strong> {c.svcBody}</p>

  <div class="anchor svc reveal pending">
    <div class="anchor-col">
      <span class="anchor-tag">{c.svcFromTag}</span>
      <dl class="svc-facts">
        {svcRows.map((r, i) => (
          <div class="svc-row">
            <dt>{svcLabels[i]}</dt>
            <dd>{tierRow('attended', r.key, lang)}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="anchor-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <div class="anchor-col">
      <span class="anchor-tag">{c.svcUnderTag}</span>
      <dl class="svc-facts">
        {svcRows.map((r, i) => (
          <div class="svc-row">
            <dt>{svcLabels[i]}</dt>
            <dd>{tierRow('unattended', r.key, lang)}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>

  {/* The gate, stated where the money is. The site never prints a date the
      capacity gate has not cleared — that rule is the reason this note exists
      on a pricing page at all. */}
  <p class="gate-note"><strong>{c.gateLead}</strong> {c.gateBody}</p>
</div></section>

<!-- THE PLANS.
     The bottom of this page used to hold a second price list (Tier 0, per
     product) for work the top of the page also sold. The ladder covers small
     counts now, so the bottom is free for the other axis: the same output,
     bought monthly, cheaper than the ladder.

     THE SAVING LINE IS NOT A MARKETING CLAIM, it is a subtraction — pricing.js
     asserts at build time that every plan costs less than the same products on
     the ladder, so a reader who does the sum finds what the card says. If that
     assertion ever fails, the fix is the price, not this copy. -->
<section class="section-tight"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-head"><h2 id="plans">{c.plansH}</h2><p>{c.plansSub}</p></div>

  <div class="packs plans-3 reveal-group">
    {planList.map((p) => (
      <div class="pack reveal pending">
        <div class="pack-top">
          <h3 class="pack-name">{p.name}</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="pack-line">{p.line}</p>

        <div class="pack-price">
          <span class="pp-amount">{p.price}</span>
          <span class="pp-unit">{p.unit}</span>
          <span class="pp-vat">{vatLabel('excl', lang)}</span>
        </div>

        <ul class="checklist pack-inc">
          {p.includes.map((f) => (
            <li><svg class="i" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg>{f}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>

        {/* The saving sentence comes out of pricing.js with two euro figures in
            it and no VAT label — it is prose, and prose cannot know which page
            it lands on. The label is appended here rather than added to the
            module, because it belongs to the surface that prints the price.
            Parenthesised so it reads as a qualifier on the sentence rather than
            as a fragment after it. */}
        {p.saving && (
          <p class="pack-saving">{p.saving} <span class="pack-saving-vat">{`(${vatLabel('excl', lang)})`}</span></p>
        )}

        <a href={lp('/contact')} class="btn btn-primary btn-block pack-cta">{c.plansCta}</a>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- TEST SAMPLE -->
<section class="section-tight"><div class="container">
  <div class="sample reveal pending">
    <div>
      <h2>{c.sampleH}</h2>
      <p class="sample-body">{c.sampleBody}</p>
      <div class="flex" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
        <a href={lp('/test-sample')} class="btn btn-primary">{c.sampleCta}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div><ProductScene icon="bottle" width="42%" badge={sample.unit} wide /></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- FAQ -->
<section class="section-tight"><div class="container narrow">
  <div class="section-head"><h2>{c.faqH}</h2></div>
  <div class="acc reveal pending">
    {c.faqs.map((f) => (
      <Disclose summary={f.q} tone="panel">
        <p>{f.a}</p>
      </Disclose>
    ))}
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- CLOSING -->
<section class="section-tight"><div class="container">
  <div class="cta-band reveal pending">
    <h2 class="display" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,3.6rem)">{c.ctaH1}<br /><em>{c.ctaEm}</em></h2>
    <div class="flex" style="justify-content:center;margin-top:2rem">
      <a href={lp('/test-sample')} class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">{c.ctaSample}</a>
      <a href={lp('/start')} class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg">{c.ctaStart}</a>
      <a href={`https://wa.me/31625436130?text=${c.waText}`} class="btn btn-wa" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <svg class="i" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M21 11.5a8.38 8.38 0 0 1-8.5 8.5 8.5 8.5 0 0 1-4-1L3 20l1.5-4.5A8.38 8.38 0 0 1 3.5 11 8.5 8.5 0 0 1 12 3a8.38 8.38 0 0 1 8.5 8.5z"/></svg>{c.ctaWa}
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<style>
  .container.narrow { max-width: 760px; }

  /* ── The ladder ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The one new device on this page, and the only place new classes were
     added. It carries a radius where the framed blocks below it do not,
     because it sits directly above the .tile grid of worked totals, which is
     16px by the system — a hard-edged frame butting a rounded one reads as
     two systems, and the table is the newer of the two. --r-md, not --r-lg:
     the tiles are the objects, the table is the surface they explain.

     The wrapper scrolls rather than the table reflowing: four columns of
     tabular numbers stacked into cards stop being comparable, which is the
     one thing this table is for. */
  .ladder-wrap {
    margin-top: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.4rem);
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
    border-radius: var(--r-md);
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .ladder {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    /* Lining, tabular: rates in a column have to line up on the decimal or
       the column stops being scannable, which is the whole reason it is a
       table and not a paragraph. */
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums lining-nums;
  }
  .ladder th, .ladder td {
    padding: clamp(.85rem, 1.6vw, 1.15rem) clamp(.9rem, 2vw, 1.5rem);
    text-align: right;
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  .ladder thead th { vertical-align: bottom; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-strong); }
  .ladder tbody tr + tr th, .ladder tbody tr + tr td { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .ladder thead th:first-child, .ladder tbody th { text-align: left; }
  .lad-corner {
    font-size: .72rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: .08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-3);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .lad-kind { display: block; font-size: .98rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--ink); }
  /* Two of the three headings are links to their pillar pages, and a link in a
     table header needs a cue at REST — a reader scanning columns is not going
     to hover each one to discover which are clickable. Hairline underline,
     thickened and accented on interaction, so the page's one accent colour
     stays on the CTA where §7 puts it. */
  .lad-kind a {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-decoration-color: var(--line-strong);
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
  }
  .lad-kind a:hover, .lad-kind a:focus-visible { text-decoration-thickness: 2px; text-decoration-color: var(--accent); }
  .lad-kind-sub {
    display: block;
    margin: .35rem 0 0 auto;
    max-width: 24ch;
    font-size: .8rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .lad-kind-vat {
    display: block;
    margin-top: .5rem;
    font-size: .68rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: .07em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-3);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .ladder tbody th { font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 700; font-size: 1rem; color: var(--ink); white-space: nowrap; }
  .lad-rate {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 1.7vw, 1.3rem);
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }
  .lad-incl { display: block; margin-top: .3rem; font-size: .75rem; color: var(--ink-3); white-space: nowrap; }

  /* ── The worked totals ───────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .worked-head { margin-top: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem); }
  .worked-head h3 { font-size: var(--t-h3); line-height: 1.2; }
  .worked-head p { margin-top: .5rem; color: var(--ink-3); font-size: .95rem; max-width: 60ch; }
  .worked { margin-top: clamp(1rem, 2vw, 1.4rem); }
  .worked-vat { margin: .5rem 0 0; font-size: .8rem; color: var(--ink-3); }

  .first-note { max-width: 78ch; }
  .ladder-cta { margin-top: clamp(1.2rem, 2.4vw, 1.6rem); }

  /* ── The anchor ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .anchor {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1px 1fr;
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
    margin-top: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 2.2rem);
  }
  .anchor-col { padding: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.4rem); display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .anchor-rule { background: var(--line-strong); }
  .anchor-tag {
    font-size: .72rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: .08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .anchor-price {
    display: block;
    margin: .5rem 0 .35rem;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3.4vw, 2.5rem);
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }
  /* The VAT line sits under the figure rather than beside it: at this type
     size a trailing "excl. VAT" would either shrink the price or wrap, and the
     label has to be readable — an unreadable VAT label is the same failure as
     no VAT label. */
  .anchor-vat { display: block; margin-bottom: .6rem; font-size: .78rem; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .anchor-meta { font-size: .9rem; color: var(--ink-3); line-height: 1.5; max-width: 34ch; min-height: 3em; }
  .anchor-list { list-style: none; margin: 1.1rem 0 0; padding: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .55rem; }
  .anchor-list li { position: relative; padding-left: 1.25rem; font-size: .92rem; color: var(--ink-2); line-height: 1.5; }
  .anchor-list li::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: .55em;
    width: 6px;
    height: 6px;
    background: var(--ink-faint);
  }
  .anchor-per { margin-top: auto; padding-top: 1.2rem; font-size: .88rem; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .anchor-note { max-width: 74ch; margin-top: 1.4rem; color: var(--ink-3); font-size: .95rem; line-height: 1.6; }
  /* The /compare link inside the note arrives through set:html and carries no
     scope attribute, so the descendant needs :global() to be reached. */
  .anchor-note :global(a) { color: var(--accent-bright); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; }

  /* ── What rides on top ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Two shapes, because two claims. The add-on is a price with a drawing
     underneath, because what is interesting about it is arithmetic and
     arithmetic is a thing you show. The clip is a price beside a list, because
     what is interesting about it is where the rate does NOT change, and that
     is a list. Neither layout would work for the other product, which is the
     test this section previously failed by rendering both from one .map(). */
  .addon {
    margin-top: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 2.2rem);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;
    align-items: start;
    gap: clamp(1.1rem, 2.6vw, 1.9rem) clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 4rem);
    padding: clamp(1.6rem, 3.2vw, 2.6rem);
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
  }
  /* A grid item, so it needs the min-width reset: the default `min-width:auto`
     makes a 1fr track refuse to shrink below its longest word, and the longest
     word here is a product name that gets translated. Without this, a long NL
     name pushes the price column off the right edge instead of wrapping. */
  .addon-id { min-width: 0; }
  .addon-name { font-size: var(--t-h3); line-height: 1.2; }
  .addon-line { margin-top: .55rem; color: var(--ink-3); font-size: .95rem; max-width: 34ch; }
  .addon-price { display: flex; align-items: baseline; justify-content: flex-end; gap: .55rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .ap-amount {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 2.8vw, 2.2rem);
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }
  .ap-unit { font-size: .86rem; color: var(--ink-3); }
  /* Full-width in the flex row, so the VAT pair always lands on its own line
     under the figure instead of wrapping unpredictably between the two. */
  .ap-vat, .sp-vat, .pp-vat { flex: 0 0 100%; font-size: .78rem; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .addon-price .ap-vat { text-align: right; }

  /* Full width, under both the name and the price, because the figure is about
     the relationship between them: it is that price, cut into the parts that
     repay it. Boxing it into one column would make it an illustration beside
     the claim rather than the claim itself. */
  .addon-fig { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
  .addon-cta { grid-column: 1 / -1; justify-self: start; display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: .4rem; }

  /* Two panels inside one frame, split by a border rather than a gap: the
     clip's rate and where that rate holds are one offer, and a gap between
     them would read as two. Same framing device as .packs, at a different
     rhythm — one row, not three columns. */
  .standing {
    margin-top: clamp(1rem, 2.4vw, 1.6rem);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
  }
  .standing-id, .standing-inc { padding: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.4rem); }
  .standing-id { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .standing-inc { border-left: 1px solid var(--line-strong); }
  .standing-name { font-size: var(--t-h3); line-height: 1.2; }
  .standing-name a {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-decoration-color: var(--line-strong);
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
  }
  .standing-name a:hover, .standing-name a:focus-visible { text-decoration-thickness: 2px; text-decoration-color: var(--accent); }
  .standing-line { margin-top: .55rem; color: var(--ink-3); font-size: .95rem; max-width: 34ch; }
  .standing-price { margin-top: 1.1rem; display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: .55rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .sp-amount {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 2.8vw, 2.2rem);
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }
  .sp-unit { font-size: .86rem; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .standing-cta { margin-top: auto; padding-top: 1.2rem; align-self: start; display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: .4rem; }

  /* Sentence case and quiet. The copy this carries is "How it is priced:",
     which is a sentence with a colon in it; setting a sentence in tracked
     uppercase is how a label stops being readable and starts being decoration,
     and DESIGN.md rejects that mark anyway. */
  .standing-inc-h { display: block; font-size: .9rem; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .standing-list { list-style: none; margin: .9rem 0 0; padding: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .55rem; }
  .standing-list li { position: relative; padding-left: 1.25rem; font-size: .93rem; color: var(--ink-2); line-height: 1.5; }
  .standing-list li::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: .55em;
    width: 6px;
    height: 6px;
    background: var(--ink-faint);
  }

  /* ── In every order ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .incl-grid {
    list-style: none;
    margin: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.4rem) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr));
    gap: 0 clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 3rem);
  }
  .incl {
    padding: 1rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: .95rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .svc-lead { margin-top: clamp(1.8rem, 3vw, 2.4rem); max-width: 78ch; color: var(--ink-2); font-size: .95rem; line-height: 1.6; }
  .svc-lead strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 700; }
  .svc { margin-top: clamp(1rem, 2vw, 1.4rem); }
  .svc-facts { margin: 1rem 0 0; }
  .svc-row { padding: .8rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .svc-row dt {
    font-size: .72rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: .08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .svc-row dd { margin: .3rem 0 0; font-size: .93rem; color: var(--ink-2); line-height: 1.5; }

  .gate-note {
    margin-top: clamp(1.8rem, 3vw, 2.4rem);
    padding: 1.05rem 1.25rem;
    /* Neutral here, unlike the same note on /start. The system palette
       (--signal) is scoped to /start, the client portal and system surfaces —
       /pricing is a marketing surface, so the note earns its emphasis from the
       rule and the bold lead rather than from hue. It would also fail contrast
       against ink on this dark ground. */
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
    font-size: .95rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    max-width: 78ch;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .gate-note strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 700; }

